	"sifCedsStandardPlugin": {
		// THE SIF LINES (B4, TWILIGHT_VALLEY, 2026-08-17). Same four-line shape as the crosswalk's, over the
		// SIF plugin's OWN store under system/dataStores/bridgeAcceptance/sif/ — the SIF producer never opens
		// the Ed-Fi decision store, and the surest way to guarantee that is for it not to know the path
		// (the same reasoning ruling 11.5 applied to the derived producer).
		//
		// THE FOUR LINES: rejudgeDebug FREEZES a block under the digest debug judge (free, no Opus, and the
		// line every gate run uses); rejudgeRealLimit is the REAL-CLIENT CONFORMANCE BATCH of ten subjects
		// (the named step the B3 DEVLOG's real-client lesson requires BEFORE any full spend — two real judge
		// contract defects were found that way and no hermetic gate could have shown either); materialiseReal
		// is the full real freeze; materialise REPLAYS the pair's latest frozen block with ZERO judge calls.
		//
		// PER-LINE JUDGMENT CEILINGS (RULING §11.12), enforced by the runner at the boundary. The projected
		// judged share is ~327 subjects (SPEC §11's measured 1,875 / 327 / 29), so 800 leaves room for a
		// re-ask on well over half of them and still halts long before a storm; the batch line's 30 leaves a
		// re-ask on every one of its ten and its second violation. As the derived block's comment says at
		// length: these are a contract the RUNNER enforces, not yet a ceiling inside the run, because the
		// only per-line channel is the command line and build.js owns flag parsing — and build.js is a seam
		// file this order may not touch. The framework's own MAX_JUDGMENT_COUNT_PER_RUN remains the
		// last-resort halt. Saying so is the point; a cap everyone believes in and nothing enforces is worse
		// than no cap at all.
		//
		// TQ'S STANDING WORD, carried through SABLE_RIVER 2026-08-17: no FULL real-judge run on SIF until the
		// limited batch has run AND TQ has seen the numbers. materialiseRealSpendAuthorisedBy stays NULL
		// until then, and the runner refuses every spending line while it is null — that refusal is the only
		// thing standing between a typo and roughly 327 Opus calls.
		//
		// THE STORE IS FORGED FRESH — RULED SABLE_RIVER 2026-08-17, option (c). NOT a copy of the Ed-Fi
		// pinned store. That store was inspected read-only before the ruling and holds TEN blocks, not four:
		// the four bases AND SIX Ed-Fi relationship blocks (subject `ceds@14.0.0.0_rel_edfi@5.2.0_exact`,
		// ~45 MB together). Copying it wholesale would put mapping blocks into the SIF store that cannot be
		// shown to be inert, and "probably unused" is not something to freeze an acceptance census on top of;
		// copying-then-deleting them would leave a graph whose provenance takes a paragraph to explain, and
		// the freeze is the wrong place for a paragraph. Forging fresh costs four forges and buys an artifact
		// that needs no explaining.
		//
		// expectedBaseBlockIdBySubject IS PINNED BELOW, and it is the thing that makes the fresh forge
		// PROVABLE rather than merely clean: the four base ids MUST come out EQUAL to these literals — the
		// same four the Ed-Fi entry pins, because it is the same four-forge content — and the runner's
		// -verify REFUSES BY NAME otherwise. That equality is the whole argument that this store holds the
		// same bases as the Ed-Fi one WITHOUT having imported a single Ed-Fi mapping block. The values are
		// not a guess: they are the ruled contract the run has to satisfy, stated before the run so the run
		// is gated by them rather than described by them afterwards.
		//
		// STILL NULL-AND-HONEST UNTIL CP2: fourBaseManifestId, and the census fixture's NAME, are taken from
		// the manifest the CP2 run actually PRODUCES (RULED: "name the census fixture by the graphId your
		// CP2 run produces"). Absent rather than guessed.
		// THE PATHS BELOW NAME THE WORKTREE, NOT system/code — AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The B4 plugin and its
		// recipes exist ONLY on branch b4-sif-plugin in system/codeWorktrees/b4sif/, because GRANITE_VALLEY is
		// the sole writer of the main tree. A line naming system/code would run a graphBuilder whose registry
		// has no sifCedsStandardPlugin and read a recipe that is not there — refused by name, correctly. The
		// runner CAUGHT THIS: it composes the graphBuilder path from its own tree root, compared it against the
		// committed line, and REFUSED the launch on the mismatch. That refusal is the gate working; the line was
		// wrong, copied from the Ed-Fi entry without accounting for the worktree.
		//
		// WHAT THE MERGE HAS TO DO, and why it costs nothing: rewrite the nine
		// `system/codeWorktrees/b4sif/educoreForge/` prefixes in this block to `system/code/educoreForge/`.
		// THE FROZEN BLOCK IDS DO NOT MOVE UNDER THAT REWRITE, and that is a code fact rather than a hope:
		// decisionBlock.js:193 computes frameworkFingerprint over "label + RELATIVE path + contents, sorted",
		// so the framework hashes the same whichever tree holds it; and the canonical frozen text carries "no
		// timestamps, run ids or paths" by construction (decisionBlock.js:10). The command STRING changes; the
		// artifact does not. A post-merge run of the rewritten line reproduces the same ids, which is exactly
		// what the three-run replay protocol asserts — so the rewrite is checkable rather than trusted.
		"standardKey": "sif",
		"recipePath": "/Users/tqwhite/Documents/webdev/educoreForge/system/codeWorktrees/b4sif/educoreForge/recipes/fourWithHubSifBridge.recipe.jsonc",
		"storeFilePath": "/Users/tqwhite/Documents/webdev/educoreForge/system/dataStores/bridgeAcceptance/sif/sifBridge.standardsDatabase.sqlite3",
		"decisionStoreFilePath": "/Users/tqwhite/Documents/webdev/educoreForge/system/dataStores/bridgeAcceptance/sif/sifBridge.decisions.sqlite3",
		"judgmentCacheFilePath": "/Users/tqwhite/Documents/webdev/educoreForge/system/dataStores/bridgeAcceptance/sif/sifBridge.judgmentCache.sqlite3",
